Subject: [PATCH] libcamera: describe the Lenovo S5KC505A sensor and gain

Adapt Mei Tao's S5KC505A gain helper as distributed by rickx, adding
Lenovo-derived sensor properties. The analogue gain is code/32 and the
RAW10 black pedestal is 64, represented as 4096 in libcamera's 16-bit
domain.

Use the 1.4 um pixel pitch, test-pattern register values and two-frame
pipeline delay documented in jethac's Lenovo sensor-library decode.
Applying that pipeline delay to individual controls is the existing
vendor-based approximation, not an independently measured latency.

The companion ThinkSmart device profile in config/s5kc505a.yaml uses the
five CCMs at 2300, 2850, 4000, 5000 and 6500 K from rickx's numeric
s5kc505a_tuning.json, without transposition or empirical colour
adjustment. Those values were decoded from Lenovo CD-18781Y210228.109
libchromatix_s5kc505a_*.so, chromatix version 0x0309. The original
source notices remain applicable; this does not assert redistribution
rights over proprietary libraries.

The profile also uses the four shared firmware LSC sets at 2300, 2850,
4000 and 6500 K. tools/generate-s5kc505a-tuning.py converts the 13x17
vendor rows to 17x17, retains register-domain fractions for subsequent
mode-crop resampling, and averages Gr/Gb because the upstream post-
demosaic renderer has one green gain. The maximum vendor Gr/Gb relative
mismatch is 1.6226%; averaging is a bounded approximation, not a
reconstruction of four-channel Qualcomm rolloff.

Keep these meshes unrotated. Rickx rotates them 180 degrees for its H/V
flips, whereas the Lenovo sensor start sequence and our driver use
register 0x0101 = 0. Android's 270-degree mounting metadata is not a
mesh rotation. Patch 0010 implements the normalized active-area crops
and texture-node mapping and supports all shared vendor gains without
byte clipping. The profile uses scale 255/59 rather than the lossy
gain*64 conversion.

The committed reference JSON has no unit-specific OTP or residual. Those
shared firmware values are not a donor-unit residual calibration; no
evidence here establishes whether different units have equal or
different residual shading. The converter reads only the explicitly
supplied JSON and never searches for a residual file. Shared firmware
references and synthetic checks do not establish a physical flat-field
error bound.

At the four reference illuminants, comparing the mode-resampled and
rounded 8-bit mesh against the original shared three-channel mesh bounds
conversion/crop/quantization gain error at 2.568% across the supported
analogue crops. This excludes Gr/Gb averaging and does not establish a
physical calibration tolerance. Upstream colour-temperature
interpolation and its 100 K update threshold are retained, not claimed
to reproduce Qualcomm's illuminant policy.

Use Rickx's empirical relativeLuminanceTarget 0.12 as the initial AGC
policy, overriding upstream's default 0.16. This is a 25% lower scene-
linear mean-luminance target, not a Lenovo factory AEC value or a
guaranteed 25% change in displayed brightness. The older TI stack uses a
different histogram-based exposure policy; this is a comparison within
the new upstream implementation.

Do not import per-unit OTP/residuals, unverified vendor AWB or gamma
translations, stretchGain 4, a global 100 ms IPA exposure cap, or custom
highlight, white-balance and hot-pixel heuristics. The Android HAL
separately supports the device configuration's chosen 10-30 FPS range;
this is not vendor optical tuning. Retain upstream AWB and Adjust and
the upstream AGC algorithms apart from that explicit target. The vendor
gamma14 array is a full tone curve, not interchangeable with the scalar
Gamma control. Copying vendor coefficients is not proof of colour
accuracy or reproduction of Qualcomm's algorithms.

